d among all the qualified people  people who also happen to be people who  were diverse and so there never was a  sign on my door saying you're a black  student or that you're an affirmative  action admit they said that you still  have to perform like everyone else to  get in here you still have to take every  single exam to pass and you still have  to meet all the requirements to leave  here and that's exactly what I did  graduating Phi Beta Kappa in three years  now this picture right here is from  where oh boy that's my graduation at  Stanford June 1975 it's a bittersweet  occasion because we learned that spring  as we're about to graduate that the  president the university had selected  Daniel Moynihan as a commencement  speaker we were troubled by that because  dentalman dunya moana hadn't liked the  melting pot and other things that we  thought were disparaging in some  respects of african-american families  that particularly for American mothers  and we thought many of us by then my  mother and father had been separated  were divorced by 1975 and there are a  number of single parents who had raised  many of the young people who were there  at Stanford with me and and we thought  our parents had done well and we didn't  think it was an appropriate way for us  to celebrate our most important day in  life that is graduating from Stanford to  Moynihan is a symbol and so what we did  we wrote our parents we black suit and  sat down and put together led and told  our parents to come to Stanford we're  going to have a separate graduation  because we wanted them to appreciate  what they had done their sacrifices they  came a day early  we had Sinclair Drake who was my mentor  and advisor he was a professor who had  been a ruler at university he'd written  the book called black metropolis great  man often had a cigarette in his hand  riding the bicycle across the Stanford  campus a pen Africanist one of my great  heroes he spoke that night Saturday  night to our paren